\details{
\strong{Model (Section 2.3, Appendix A).} For one stratum:
\enumerate{
\item the death rates of each year give life-table deaths \code{d[x, t]} with
radix 1 at the first age (\code{\link[=life_table]{life_table()}} conventions);
\item \code{a[x]} is the geometric mean of \code{d[x, t]} over the years, closed to 1;
\item the deaths are centred by perturbation (\code{d[x, t] / a[x]}, closed) and
transformed by the centred log-ratio (clr), eq. (5);
\item the first component of a singular value decomposition gives
\verb{clr(d[x, t] / a[x]) = b[x] k[t]}, eq. (6); \code{b} and \code{k} sum to zero, and
their signs make \code{k} increase over the fitting period (p. 533);
\item \code{k[t]} follows an ARIMA(0,1,1) model with drift, fitted by maximum
likelihood (\code{order}, \code{drift}; p. 536 and Appendix A, p. 555, where this
order had the best BIC for most of 15 countries);
\item forecasts are transformed back (inverse clr, perturbation by \code{a},
closure, eq. 7) to life-table deaths and then to death rates.
}

\strong{Jump-off (Appendix G, p. 566).} With \code{jump_off = "observed"} (the
default), forecasts are the life-table deaths of the last year \code{T}
perturbed by the change of the model (eq. 17): \code{d[x, T + j]} is
proportional to \verb{d[x, T] exp(b[x] (k[T + j] - k[T]))}. With \code{"fitted"},
forecasts start from the fitted deaths of year \code{T}. The point forecast of
the ARIMA(0,1,1) index with drift is shifted so that its first step equals
the drift (eq. 20), which removes the break that the moving-average term
causes in the first forecast year; the package applies the same
correction to any ARIMA(0,1,q) index with drift.

\strong{Coherent model (Section 2.4, Appendix C).} The common component
\verb{B[x] K[t]} is the model above fitted to the average mortality of a group:
the arithmetic mean of the death rates of its strata (equal weights, p.
557), turned into life-table deaths. Each stratum \code{i} has its own centre
\code{a[x, i]} and deviation \verb{clr(d[x, t, i] / a[x, i] / C[exp(B K)]) = b[x, i] k[t, i]} (eq. 8a), the first component of the SVD after removing the
common component. \code{K} follows the model of \code{order} and \code{drift} (with eq.
20), each \code{k[t, i]} an ARIMA(1,1,0) model without drift (\code{order_dev},
\code{drift_dev}; p. 537), whose point forecast levels off, so that the point
forecasts of the strata do not diverge; their intervals still widen with
the horizon. \code{order_dev = c(1, 0, 0)} gives an AR(1) process with a mean,
which Li and Lee (2005) suggest for their deviations (as cited on p. 531)
and whose deviations stay bounded in distribution. Groups are all strata,
or each level of \code{common_by}; if the data carry a hierarchy (see
\code{\link[=mt_aggregate_strata]{mt_aggregate_strata()}}), the average uses the bottom strata only, as
\code{\link[=li_lee]{li_lee()}} does.

\strong{Draws (Appendix B, pp. 556-557).} Draws carry the uncertainty of the
estimates and of the index forecasts: the residuals of eq. (5) are
resampled cell by cell and added to the fitted component, \code{b} and \code{k} are
estimated again (\code{a} is kept, as in the paper), the ARIMA model is fitted
again to the new index, and future paths of the index are simulated with
innovations resampled from its residuals. \code{ceiling(sqrt(n_draws))} refits
share the draws (the paper uses 100 refits with 100 paths each). In the
coherent model, the common and the stratum components are simulated
independently and added (Appendix B); the draws of the common component
are the same for all strata of a group. The draws are then centred on the
point forecast: each draw is perturbed by the ratio of the point forecast
of the first forecast year to the median of the draws in that year, part
by part. This follows the authors' code (CoDaForecast.R, supplementary
material of the paper); eq. (19) of the paper shifts the bounds of the
intervals of life expectancy so that their median is the point forecast.
Draws are expected rates; predictive draws (\code{mt_forecast(predictive = TRUE)}) add Poisson variation of the deaths given the rates and the
exposures, and no other observation noise. The model weights all parts
equally on the clr scale, so the noise of ages with few deaths enters \code{k};
its intervals can be wider than those of \code{\link[=lee_carter]{lee_carter()}} (p. 543).

\strong{Old ages (Appendix C, pp. 557-558).} With single-year age groups from
80 (at least to 89) and \code{open = "auto"} (the default) or \code{"kannisto"}, the
rates from age 80 are replaced in every year by a Kannisto model (Thatcher,
Kannisto and Vaupel 1998) fitted by Poisson maximum likelihood to the
deaths and exposures of the single ages 80 to 100 of the grid (the
authors' code fits ages 80-100 of the Human Mortality Database, open
interval 110+) and applied up to age 120, where the life table closes; the
single ages 80-120 are parts of the composition. The rate of an open age interval is the forecast deaths over
the forecast person-years of its parts, and the forecasts of the ages from
80 are those of the smoothed rates. Other age groups (for example 5-year
groups) do not allow this extension. Then, with \code{open = "auto"} or
\code{"regression"}, the deaths of an open interval are the survivors at its
lower bound, and its log death rate gets loadings on the same indexes,
estimated by least squares: \verb{log m[open, t] = a_o + b_o k[t]} (coherent
model: \verb{a_o[i] + B_o K[t] + b_o[i] k[t, i]}, \code{B_o} from the log of the
average rate of the group); a closed age range adds the survivors at its
upper bound as a part. Both are package-specific (reference unknown).

\strong{Zero deaths (Appendix C, p. 558).} Zero counts of the ages that keep
their own rates (below 80 with the Kannisto extension, all ages otherwise)
are replaced multiplicatively in each year: each zero gets the share
\code{delta} of the deaths of the year, the other shares are multiplied by
\verb{1 - z delta} (\code{z} zeros), and \code{delta} is half the smallest positive count
over all ages and years divided by the deaths of the year. A warning is
given when more than 10\% of the cells are zero. Package safeguards: the
imputed share of a year is at most 1/2, and a year whose deaths at ages
from 80 allow no Kannisto fit takes the fit to all years together (with a
warning).

\strong{Deviations from the paper.} Besides the package-specific treatment of
other age groups: the life tables use \code{a_x = n / 2} at every age (the
authors' code uses 0.06 at age 0); the residuals of the bootstrap are
those of eq. (5), as the paper describes (the authors' code takes them
around the in-sample one-step forecasts of the ARIMA model); the number of
refits follows \code{n_draws}; the draws are centred as in the authors' code
(see above).
